"Chebyshev polynomials over finite fields and reversibility of σ-automata on square grids - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0304397504001823)\nciteturn6search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 22.4 years ago; Crawled: last month; Date: 14 June 2004 ... Author links open overlay panel Markus Hunziker ^{a}, António Machiavelo ^{b} ^{1}, Jihun Park ^{c} ... View PDF Download full issue ... Using number theory on function fields and algebraic number fields, we prove results about Chebyshev polynomials over finite prime fields to investigate reversibility of two-dimensional additive cellular automata on finite square grids.\n\nDate: 14 June 2004\n\nPages: 465-483\n\nVolume: Volume 320, Issues 2–3\n\n## Published by: Elsevier\n\n# Chebyshev polynomials over finite fields and reversibility of σ-automata on square grids\n\nAuthor links open overlay panel Markus Hunziker ^{a}, António Machiavelo ^{b} ^{1}, Jihun Park ^{c}\n\nShow more\n\nView PDF Download full issue\n\nCite\n\nAdd to Mendeley\n\nShare\n\n10.1016/j.tcs.2004.03.031\n\n## Abstract\n\nUsing number theory on function fields and algebraic number fields, we prove results about Chebyshev polynomials over finite prime fields to investigate reversibility of two-dimensional additive cellular automata on finite square grids. For example, we show that there are infinitely many primitive irreversible additive cellular automata on square grids when the base field has order two or three.\n\n## Keywords\n\nAdditive cellular automata\n\n;\n\nChebyshev polynomials\n\n;\n\nFinite fields\n\n^{1}--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nResolution to Sutner's Conjecture (https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.09878)\nciteturn6academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.7 years ago; Title: Resolution to Sutner's Conjecture\nAuthors: William Boyles\nTitle: Resolution to Sutner's Conjecture\nAuthors: William Boyles\nDate: Sun Feb 20 17:59:03 2022\n\nConsider a game played on a simple graph $G = (V,E)$ where each vertex consists of a clickable light. Clicking any vertex $v$ toggles the on/off state of $v$ and its neighbors. One wins the game by finding a sequence of clicks that turns off all the lights. When $G$ is a $5 \\times 5$ grid, this game was commercially available from Tiger Electronics as Lights Out. Sutner was one of the first to study these games mathematically. He found that when $d(G) = \\text{dim}(\\text{ker}(A + I))$ over the field $GF(2)$, where $A$ is the adjacency matrix of $G$, is 0 all initial configurations are solvable. When investigating $n \\times n$ grid graphs, Sutner conjectured that $d_{2n+1} = 2d_{n} + \\delta_{n}, \\delta_{n} \\in \\{0,2\\}, \\delta_{2n+1} = \\delta_{n}$, where $d_n = d(G)$ for $G$ an $n \\times n$ grid graph. We resolve this conjecture in the affirmative. We use results from Sutner that give $d_n$ as the GCD of two polynomials in the ring $\\mathbb{Z}_2[x]$. We then apply identities from Hunziker, Machiavelo, and Park that relate the polynomials of $(2n+1) \\times (2n+1)$ grids and $n \\times n$ grids. Finally, we use a result from Ore about the GCD of two products. Together these results allow us to prove Sutner's conjecture. We then go further and show for exactly which values of $n$ $\\delta_n$ is 0 or 2.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Effect of Data Dimensionality on Neural Network (https://openreview.net/references/pdf?id=eMiJHzPtVf)\nciteturn6search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.1 years ago; The Effect of Data Dimensionality on Neural Network ... further conjecture that the mismatch between the intrinsic dimensionality and the minimum number\nThe Effect of Data Dimensionality on Neural Network\nPrunability\nAnonymous Author(s)\nAffiliation\nAddress\nemail\nAbstract\nPractitioners often prune neural networks for efficiency gains and generalization\n1\nimprovements, but few scrutinize the factors determining the prunability of a neural\n2\nnetwork – the maximum fraction of weights that pruning can remove without\n3\ncompromising the model’s test accuracy. In this work, we study the properties of\n4\ninput data that may contribute to the prunability of a neural network.\n5\nFor high dimensional input data such as images, text, and audio, the manifold\n6\nhypothesis suggests that these high dimensional inputs actually lie on or near\n7\na significantly lower dimensional manifold. Prior work demonstrates that the\n8\nunderlying low dimensional structure of the input data may affect the sample\n9\nefficiency of learning. In this paper, we investigate whether the low dimensional\n10\nstructure of the input data affects the prunability of a neural network.\n11\n1\nIntroduction\n12\nThe manifold hypothesis states that the input data for tasks involving images, texts and sounds lie in a\n13\nlow-dimensional manifold embedded in a high dimensional space [Fefferman et al., 2016]. Ample\n14\nevidence [Cayton, 2005, Narayanan and Mitter, 2010, Ham et al., 2004] supports the manifold\n15\nhypothesis for commonly used datasets in machine learning tasks. The manifold hypothesis seems\n16\nto also connect to the sample efficiency of deep neural network models [Narayanan and Niyogi,\n17\n2009, Pope et al., 2021] – with just a few thousand samples per label category, models learn to\n18\nclassify natural images residing in approximately one hundred thousand dimensional pixel space with\n19\nhuman-level accuracy [Simonyan and Zisserman, He et al., Szegedy et al., 2016]. We believe that the\n20\nlow-dimensional manifold structure of input data may also play a role in the surprising empirical\n21\nsuccess of weight pruning – pruning techniques can remove more than 90% of weights in a neural\n22\nnetwork model without compromising its accuracy [LeCun et al., Frankle and Carbin, Han et al.].\n23\nIn this work, we empirically examine the impact of input data dimensionality on the prunability\n24\nof a neural network model, defined as the maximum fraction of weights that a pruning technique\n25\ncan remove without reducing model accuracy. We refer to the dimensionality of the raw data as its\n26\nextrinsic dimensionality, and the dimensionality of the lower dimensional manifold that the data\n27\nlies on as its intrinsic dimensionality. We conjecture that the mismatch between the extrinsic and\n28\nintrinsic dimensionality of the input data contributes to the prunability of neural network models. We\n29\nfurther conjecture that the mismatch between the intrinsic dimensionality and the minimum number\n30\nof intrinsic dimensions that the task being learned depends on, referred to as the task dimensionality,\n31\naffects prunability as well. Guided by our conjectures, we develop and test three hypotheses: that\n32\nthe extrinsic, intrinsic and task dimensionality each contributes to the prunability of neural network\n33\nmodels. We independently vary the extrinsic, intrinsic, and task dimensionalities and observe their\n34\neffect on the prunability of the neural network model.\n35\nSubmitted to 36th Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2022). Do not distribute.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[16] M. Hunziker, A. Machiavelo, J. Park, Chebyshev polynomials over finite fields and reversibility of σ-automata on square grids, *Theoret. Comput. Sci.*, **320** (2004), 465–483. MR (https://distantreader.org/tmp/carrels-ojs/pump/cache/pump-2593.pdf)\nciteturn6search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.2 years ago; Hunziker, A. Machiavelo, J. Park, Chebyshev polynomials over finite fields and reversibility of σ-automata on square grids, *Theoret. ... *, **320** (2004), 465–483.\n[16] M. Hunziker, A. Machiavelo, J. Park, Chebyshev polynomials over finite fields and reversibility of σ-automata on square grids, *Theoret. Comput. Sci.*, **320** (2004), 465–483. MR\n\n[17] B.D. McKay, Combinatorial Data, available online at