e read from the associated click matrix\n[AF98]. Later work broadened this perspective in several directions. Hunziker, Machiavelo, and\nPark related reversibility on square grids to Chebyshev polynomials over finite fields [HMP04],\nwhich is the source of the rank-deficiency criterion used below. Kreh studied board-size and multi-\nstate variants using linear algebra and algebraic number theory [Kre17]; Torrence isolated “easy”\nsquare and toroidal games, where pressing precisely the initially lit buttons solves the board [Tor11].\nThere is also a substantial graph-theoretic literature. Fleischer and Yu give a survey that unifies\nresults from graph theory, recreational puzzle solving, and algorithms [FY13].\nBerman, Borer,\nand Hungerb¨uhler formulate Lights Out on general graphs using a Fredholm-type alternative and\nseparating invariants [BBH21]. Related variants include lit-only games, in which only lit vertices\nmay be played; these lead to minimum-light-number questions on grids and other graph classes\n[GWW11, Hua15].\nThe present note focuses on the classical square grid, but the same linear\nstructure is what makes these wider connections possible.\nWe generalize to n × n boards. Such boards are the matrices in the abelian group\nMn×n(Z2),\nwhere addition is matrix addition modulo 2. Zeros represent dark squares and ones represent lit\nsquares. The zero matrix is the dark board, and every board is its own inverse.\nLet [n] = {1, . . . , n}. Buttons are indexed by ordered pairs in [n] × [n].\n2--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe pleated bowtie spectral-rank conjecture | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/353438/the-pleated-bowtie-spectral-rank-conjecture)\nciteturn6search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; This claim extends the proved case $t=8$t=8 established immediately before the conjecture; its status for all $t\\geq 8$t≥8 is not resolved in the supplied text. ... Clark, Felipe Thomaz and Andrew Stephen, “On the Effect of Data Dimensionality on Eigenvector Centrality”, arXiv:2201.12034 (2022).\n\n# The pleated bowtie spectral-rank conjecture\n\nAbout 4 years old · traced to 2022\n\nLet $B_t$B t​ denote the $t$t-pleated bowtie graph, and let $\\mathfrak{u}(B_t)$u(B t​) be its spectral rank. Pleated bowtie conjecture. We have\n\n$$\\mathfrak{u}(B_t)=1$$u(B t​)=1\n\nfor $t\\geq 8$t≥8. This claim extends the proved case $t=8$t=8 established immediately before the conjecture; its status for all $t\\geq 8$t≥8 is not resolved in the supplied text.\n\nReferences\n\n## Primary source\n\nGregory J. Clark, Felipe Thomaz and Andrew Stephen, “On the Effect of Data Dimensionality on Eigenvector Centrality”, arXiv:2201.12034 (2022).\n\nhypergraphsspectral-graph-theory\n\n0 0\n\nPosted 1 month ago\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n## Solutions 0\n\n[Button: Post a solution]\n\n[Button: Solve with an agent]\n\nNo solutions have been posted yet.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nÜber rhombische Netzeinteilung auf Flächen (https://documentsdelivered.com/source/040/730/040730581.php)\nciteturn6search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Chebyshev investigated equidistant grids on curved surfaces with E=G=1,F=cosα for the first time. ... Part B contains miscellaneous topics on geodesic-rhombic grids, and in part C the biographies of Chebyshev, Darboux, Voss, Finsterwalder and Liebmann are given.\n\n# Über rhombische Netzeinteilung auf Flächen\n\n##### Volk, O.\n\n* * *\n\n#### Zeitschrift für angewandte Mathematik und Physik ZAMP 30(2): 374-387\n\nISSN/ISBN: 0041-008X\n\n* * *\n\n#### 1979\n\n* * *\n\nDOI: 10.1007/bf01601949\nDocument: 040730581\n\nOne hundred years ago P. L. Chebyshev investigated equidistant grids on curved surfaces with E=G=1,F=cosα for the first time. In part A of this paper we report about subsequent investigations, in particular about rhombic grids (E=G) formed by curves with constant geodesic curvature. Part B contains miscellaneous topics on geodesic-rhombic grids, and in part C the biographies of Chebyshev, Darboux, Voss, Finsterwalder and Liebmann are given.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTchebyshev Posets (pdf) | Paperity (https://paperity.org/p/11672341/tchebyshev-posets)\nciteturn6search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 22.2 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; This is a preview of a remote PDF: https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s00454-004-1115-5.pdf ... Tchebyshev Posets, Discrete & Computational Geometry, 2004, pp.\nThis is a preview of a remote PDF: https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s00454-004-1115-5.pdf\nArticle home page: http://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00454-004-1115-5\n\nHetyei, Gábor. Tchebyshev Posets, Discrete & Computational Geometry, 2004, pp. 493-520, Volume 32, Issue 4, DOI: 10.1007/s00454-004-1115-5--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRectangular and hexagonal grids used for observation, experiment (https://paperzz.com/doc/8284647/rectangular-and-hexagonal-grids-used-for-observation--exp...)\nciteturn6search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 19.5 years ago; Crawled: last month; The regular rectangular or square grid is used more often than ...     Cells in a rectangular grid can also easily be combined to produce new grids with lower res- ...     Square lattice ...     0304-3800/$ – see front matter © 2007 Elsevier B.V. ...     12,600 locations at 27 km intervals nationwide (Hunsaker et ...     Sahr, K., White, D., Kimerling, A.J., 2003. ...     Assessment of environmental correlates with the distribution\n### Rectangular and hexagonal grids used for observation, experiment\n    \n    e c o l o g i c a l m o d e l l i n g 2 0 6 ( 2 0 0 7 ) 347–359\n    available at www.sciencedirect.com\n    journal homepage: www.elsevier.com/locate/ecolmodel\n    Rectangular and hexagonal grids used for observation,\n    experiment and simulation in ecology\n    Colin P.D. Birch a,∗ , Sander P. Oom b , Jonathan A. Beecham c\n    a\n    b\n    c\n    Veterinary Laboratories Agency-Weybridge, New Haw, Addlestone KT15 3NB, UK\n    DHV B.V., PO Box 1132, 3800 BC Amersfoort, The Netherlands\n    Centre for Environment, Fisheries and Aquaculture Science, Pakeﬁeld Road, Lowestoft, Suffolk NR33 0HT, UK\n    a r t i c l e\n    i n f o\n    a b s t r a c t\n    Article history:\n    Regular grids or lattices are frequently used to study ecosystems, for observations, exper-\n    Received 17 March 2005\n    iments and simulations. The regular rectangular or square grid is used more often than\n    Received in revised form\n    the hexagonal grid, but their relative merits have been little discussed.     centre of a square with unit area is 0.3826, whereas the average distance from the centre of a hexagon with the same area\n    is 0.3772. This difference could be equalized by covering an\n    area with just 2.9% more squares than hexagons. Nevertheless, Overton et al. (1990) presented these small quantitative\n    advantages, along with “an extra degree of freedom from coincidence with anthropogenic structures”, as being the reasons\n    for preferring a hexagonal grid for the Environmental Mapping\n    and Assessment Project (EMAP) in the USA. The EMAP data set\n    is based on a random systematic hexagonal grid identifying\n    12,600 locations at 27 km intervals nationwide (Hunsaker et\n    al., 1994; Hale et al., 1998).\n    3.2.\n    Field survey\n    Here we distinguish survey from sampling by considering\n    survey to be the observation of measures, such as vegetation cover, over a whole area. Dale (1998) believed hexagonal\n    quadrats provided few advantages for ﬁeld observations compared with the extra difﬁculty of laying them out. However,\n    many of the advantages from using hexagonal grids in surveys\n    are due to arranging quadrats in a hexagonal lattice, rather\n    than the hexagonal quadrat itself. The gaps between rows in\n    √\n    a hexagonal lattice are 3/2 (ca. 0.866) of the distance between\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDaily Papers - Hugging Face (https://huggingface.co/papers?q=high-dimensional)\nciteturn6search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago; ### The Effect of Data Dimensionality on Neural Network Prunability ... For high dimensional input data such as images, text, and audio, the manifold hypothesis suggests that these high dimensional inputs approximately lie on or near a significantly lower dimensional manifold.\n\n·\n\nApr 11, 2024\n\n-\n\n### The Effect of Data Dimensionality on Neural Network Prunability\n\nPractitioners prune neural networks for efficiency gains and generalization improvements, but few scrutinize the factors determining the prunability of a neural network the maximum fraction of weights that pruning can remove without compromising the model's test accuracy. In this work, we study the properties of input data that may contribute to the prunability of a neural network. For high dimensional input data such as images, text, and audio, the manifold hypothesis suggests that these high dimensional inputs approximately lie on or near a significantly lower dimensional manifold. Prior work demonstrates that the underlying low dimensional structure of the input data may affect the sample efficiency of learning. In this paper, we investigate whether the low dimensional structure of the input data affects the prunability of a neural network.\n\n5 authors\n\n·\n\nDec 1, 2022\n\n-\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA Geometric Theorem for Network Design | CaltechAUTHORS (https://authors.library.caltech.edu/records/6ys3y-24856)\nciteturn6search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 22.6 years ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Published April 1, 2004 | Version public ... Consider an infinite square grid G. ... FRAieeetc04.pdf md5:bc6e52a9a4b4f72621fb5d4e4e9e46ae  | 851.5 kB  | Preview Download\n\nPublished April 1, 2004 | Version public\n\nJournal Article Open\n\n# A Geometric Theorem for Network Design\n\n### Creators\n\n  * Franceschetti, Massimo^{1}\n  * Cook, Matthew\n  * Bruck, Jehoshua\n\n[Button: Show affiliations ]\n\n  * 1. University of California, Berkeley\n\n## Abstract\n\nConsider an infinite square grid G. How many discs of given radius r, centered at the vertices of G, are required, in the worst case, to completely cover an arbitrary disc of radius r placed on the plane? We show that this number is an integer in the set {3,4,5,6} whose value depends on the ratio of r to the grid spacing. One application of this result is to design facility location algorithms with constant approximation factors. Another application is to determine if a grid network design, where facilities are placed on a regular grid in a way that each potential customer is within a reasonably small radius around the facility, is cost effective in comparison to a nongrid design. This can be relevant to determine a cost effective design for base station placement in a wireless network.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPatrick R. Hunziker (https://www.csauthors.net/patrick-r-hunziker/)\nciteturn6search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Hunziker ... 2004\n,\n\nAndrés Santos\n\n,\n\nMichael Sühling\n\n,\n\nPatrick R. Hunziker\n\n,\n\nMichael Unser\n\nIEEE Trans. Medical Imaging, 2005\n\nMyocardial motion analysis from B-mode echocardiograms.\n\n[BibT_{e}X]\n\n[DOI]\n\nMichael Sühling\n\n,\n\nMuthuvel Arigovindan\n\n,\n\nChristian P. Jansen\n\n,\n\nPatrick R. Hunziker\n\n,\n\nMichael Unser\n\nIEEE Trans. Image Process., 2005\n\nVariational image reconstruction from arbitrarily spaced samples: a fast multiresolution splin